definition of leg before wicket lbw currently law # laws of cricket written by marylebone cricket club mcc before batsman can be dismissed lbw fielding team must appeal to umpire for batsman to be lbw ball if it must pitch line with wickets or on off side of stumps then ball must strike part of batsman body without first touching his bat line with wickets and have been going on to hit stumps batsman may also be out lbw if having made no attempt to hit ball with his bat he struck outside line of off stump by ball that would have hit wickets umpire must assume that ball would have continued on same trajectory after striking batsman even if it would have bounced before hitting stumps however if bowler delivers no ball an illegal delivery batsman cannot be lbw under any circumstances 
batsman can be out lbw even if ball did not hit his leg for example batsman struck on head could be lbw however he cannot be lbw if ball pitches on leg side of stumps outside leg stump even if ball would have otherwise hit wickets similarly batsman who has attempted to hit ball with his bat cannot be lbw if ball strikes him outside line of off stump however some shots cricket such as switch hit or reverse sweep involve batsman switching between and left handed stance this affects location of off and leg side which are determined by stance law explicitly states that off side determined by batsman position when bowler his run up 
