archaeology of ritual and magic an archaeological study of material evidence for ritual and magical practices europe containing particular emphasis on london and south east england it was written by english archaeologist ralph merrifield former deputy director of museum of london and first published by # 
merrifield opens archaeology of ritual and magic by discussing how archaeologists have understood magic and ritual practices past societies opining that on whole it had been neglected area of study looking at archaeological evidence for ritual activity pre roman iron age and roman iron age of britain he discusses animal and human sacrifice as well as offering of votive deposits rivers and other bodies of water he moves on to explore rituals surrounding death and burial suggesting areas where this ritual activity visible burial record of multiple societies merrifield goes on to discuss archaeological evidence for ritual practices christian europe highlighting areas of ritual from earlier pagan periods particular deposition of metal goods water looking at evidence for foundation deposits european buildings that likely had religious purposes he then looks at several examples of written charms and spells which have survived archaeological record 
upon publication archaeology of ritual and magic received predominantly positive reviews academic peer reviewed journals such as folklore and antiquaries journal ensuing years book has been widely cited by scholars as an influential and pioneering text study of archaeology of ritual and magic 
ralph merrifield # # was born and raised brighton and following an education at grammar school he worked at brighton museum gaining london external degree anthropology # he developed lifelong interest religious and magical beliefs of england after serving royal air force during second world war he returned to working at brighton museum but # was appointed assistant keeper of guildhall museum city of london over six month period # and # he was stationed ghana where he worked at national museum of ghana organising collection preparation for country independence from british empire march # returning to guildhall museum merrifield compiled first detailed study of roman london for # years which was published as roman city of london # following creation of museum of london # he became its deputy director post which he held until his retirement # 
preface of archaeology of ritual and magic merrifield noted that book bias was to archaeology of london and that this was particularly evident its use of illustrations he dedicated book to memory of former curator of brighton museum and one time assistant to archaeologist augustus pitt rivers his dedication merrifield noted that had been his first mentor archaeology and folk studies 
his preface merrifield noted that while archaeologists studying prehistoric periods have paid increasing attention to evidence for ritual and magic archaeological record their counterparts working later historical periods have failed to follow their lead presenting this book as he outlines his intentions and study limitations 
chapter one ritual and archaeologist begins by describing ritual deposits from pre roman iron age sites of castle and southern britain merrifield laments fact that majority of archaeologists particularly those studying literate historical periods have avoided ritual explanations for unusual phenomenon archaeological record he believes that they exhibit ritual phobia he contrasts this view with that of those archaeologists studying neolithic and bronze age britain who have widely accepted ritual uses of chambered tombs and stone circles he specifies particular definitions for words such as ritual religion and superstition arguing that such terms must be used with precision by archaeologists offering case study he describes how neolithic stone axes were adopted as or later roman iron age onward britain and that as such archaeologists should expect to find them non prehistoric contexts 
second chapter offerings to earth and water pre roman and roman times merrifield explores various forms of archaeological evidence for ritual deposits pre roman iron age and roman iron age of britain he provides an overview of evidence for animal and human sacrifice as well as that support of ritual offerings bodies of water such as river thames merrifield deals with votive deposits on land particular looking at evidence for deposition ditches shafts and wells he rounds off chapter by examining evidence for iron age rituals that took place at commencement and termination of building constructions chapter three rituals of death deals with religious rituals accompanying death and burial and their visibility archaeological record it explains three main ways which human communities have dealt with corpses of dead through exposing them to elements and scavengers through and through cremation at beliefs surrounding afterlife merrifield discusses ways which these beliefs might be visible archaeological record such as through deposition of grave goods discussing evidence for rituals of separation through which deceased separated from world of living including those that deal with of body merrifield then looks at effect of christianity on burials europe arguing that it brought new intimacy with dead through collection of relics which was contrast to pagan beliefs of roman empire which portrayed deceased as 
fourth chapter from paganism to christianity explores continuing practice of ritual christian europe discussing early roman catholic church of pagan deities merrifield states that church continued propagating form of polytheism through cult of blessed dead veneration of saints and martyrs throughout middle ages discussing ritual use of christian relics he also looks at votive offerings that were presented christian context at shrines and churches paying particular reference to tradition of offering bent coins to shrines late medieval england examining construction of churches on earlier pagan ritual sites he deals with evidence for destruction of pagan statues by early christians 
chapter five survivals revivals and continues merrifield exploration of ritual practices christian europe he examines and post medieval items that have been deposited rivers including swords and pilgrimage souvenirs speculating that their deposition might represent survival from pagan tradition of casting votive offerings into water he speculates that contemporary practice of throwing coin into fountain for good luck further survival of custom he then discusses evidence for ritual foundation deposits under buildings noting widespread medieval deposition of an animal head or jaw construction foundations presumably for protective magical purposes proceeding to deal with and post medieval deposition of pots under foundations he looks at their place churches where it was believed that they aided acoustics continuing with this theme he discusses british folk custom of burying horse head under floor to improve building acoustics speculating as to whether this was survival of pagan foundation deposit custom rounding off chapter merrifield examines at magical items that have been intentionally placed walls chimneys and roofs of buildings britain particular widespread use of dead cats and old shoes 
sixth chapter written spells and charms merrifield discusses use of written word magical contexts highlighting archaeological examples from ancient roman world he looks at inscriptions on lead tablets that were buried and both places associated with dead moving on to use of magic squares merrifield highlights various examples of square archaeological contexts before also discussing squares that contained numerical data with significance he rounds off chapter with an examination of post medieval and charms containing written word citing examples that have been found by archaeologists across britain hidden inside various parts of buildings seventh chapter charms against witchcraft he deals with archaeological evidence for variety of early modern and modern british spells designed to ward off witchcraft after briefly discussing role of holed stone charms he looks at evidence for witch bottles making reference to their relation to beliefs about witches proceeding to focus on and century examples merrifield discusses case of james an english cunning man and his involvement with witch bottle tradition merrifield final chapter ritual of superstition recognition and potential for study provides an overview of entire book highlighting evidence of ritual continuity from pre christian periods to present day pointing out what he sees as areas of further exploration for archaeologists he calls for systematic investigation of subject 
archaeology of ritual and magic was reviewed by john hutchings for folklore journal published arm of folklore society he highlighted how work would be of benefit to folklorists by putting various charms then museum exhibits such as dead cats buried shoes and witch bottles into wider context of ritual activity he opined that it was little disappointing that examples were almost all from london and home counties but described book as written carefully argued and well illustrated 
short review for antiquaries journal historian of religion hilda ellis davidson praised cautious and balanced arguments of merrifield work she opined that it should be read by every archaeologist as to what she thought was their widespread ignorance of folklore noting that wide array of evidence for ritual behaviour archaeological record would surprise many readers 
